# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 2
A(('h1', S2))
A(('p', '2.1 The scene in Matthew. Matthew 13 opens on one day: “The same day went Jesus out of the house, and sat by the sea side” (Matthew 13:1). “Great multitudes” are gathered so that He goes into a ship and sits, “and the whole multitude stood on the shore” (13:2). “He spake many things unto them in parables, saying, Behold, a sower went forth to sow” (13:3). This is the first parable Matthew records in the chapter.'))
A(('p', '2.2 The scene in Mark. Mark says, “And he began again to teach by the sea side: and there was gathered unto him a great multitude, so that he entered into a ship, and sat in the sea; and the whole multitude was by the sea on the land” (Mark 4:1). He “taught them many things by parables, and said unto them in his doctrine, Hearken; Behold, there went out a sower to sow” (4:2–3).'))
A(('p', '2.3 The scene in Luke. Luke gives a different opening. Just before it, Luke says that Jesus “went throughout every city and village, preaching and shewing the glad tidings of the kingdom of God: and the twelve were with him” (Luke 8:1). Then: “And when much people were gathered together, and were come to him out of every city, he spake by a parable” (8:4).'))
A(('p', '2.4 No question from the crowd introduces the story. In none of the three accounts does the multitude ask anything before the parable is told. The questions that belong to this parable come afterward and from the disciples. In Matthew, “the disciples came, and said unto him, Why speakest thou unto them in parables?” (13:10). In Mark, “when he was alone, they that were about him with the twelve asked of him the parable” (4:10). In Luke, “his disciples asked him, saying, What might this parable be?” (8:9). The story is told to the crowd, and the questions are asked of Jesus by those who follow Him.'))
A(('call', 'The Questions That Belong to This Parable',
   '“Why speakest thou unto them in parables?” (Matthew 13:10). “They that were about him with the twelve asked of him the parable” (Mark 4:10). “What might this parable be?” (Luke 8:9). Each question follows the telling of the story and is put to Jesus by His disciples. The story itself is told to the multitude without a question from them.'))
A(('p', '2.5 The setting before the scene. Matthew 12 closes with the opposition that stands behind chapter 13. The Pharisees “held a council against him, how they might destroy him” (Matthew 12:14), and when they heard that He cast out devils they said, “This fellow doth not cast out devils, but by Beelzebub the prince of the devils” (12:24). Matthew records the parable of the sower on the day that follows in his account, with refusal already spoken aloud and crowds still gathering.'))
A(('p', '2.6 The prophetic context. Matthew has already said what Jesus preached: “Repent: for the kingdom of heaven is at hand” (Matthew 4:17). He had sent the Twelve with a limited charge: “Go not into the way of the Gentiles, and into any city of the Samaritans enter ye not: But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (10:5–6). Luke’s own words are that He preached “the glad tidings of the kingdom of God” (Luke 8:1). The parable is spoken to people who have been told that the Kingdom is at hand, and the explanation Jesus gives speaks of “the word of the kingdom” (Matthew 13:19).'))
