# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 3
A(('h1', S3))
A(('p', 'The parable is read here in the order Jesus tells it. Matthew’s account is read first, verse by verse. Then Mark’s and Luke’s accounts are read one at a time, and the three are set side by side by wording only. Nothing from Jesus’ later explanation is placed into the story. The story is read first as a story.'))
A(('p', '3.1 The sowing begins (Matthew 13:3). “Behold, a sower went forth to sow.” The story opens with a sower who goes out for one purpose, to sow. The word “Behold” asks the hearers to look at what follows.'))
A(('p', '3.2 The way side (13:4). “And when he sowed, some seeds fell by the way side, and the fowls came and devoured them up.” Some seeds fall by the way side. The fowls come, and the seeds are devoured. The verse does not say that the seeds sprang up.'))
A(('p', '3.3 The stony places (13:5–6). “Some fell upon stony places, where they had not much earth: and forthwith they sprung up, because they had no deepness of earth: And when the sun was up, they were scorched; and because they had no root, they withered away.” Here the seeds do spring up, and quickly, because the earth is not deep. Then the sun rises, they are scorched, and they wither. Two reasons are given in the verse itself: no deepness of earth, and no root.'))
A(('p', '3.4 Among thorns (13:7). “And some fell among thorns; and the thorns sprung up, and choked them.” The seeds fall in a place where thorns are, the thorns spring up, and the seeds are choked. The verse does not say whether the seeds came up first.'))
A(('p', '3.5 The good ground (13:8). “But other fell into good ground, and brought forth fruit, some an hundredfold, some sixtyfold, some thirtyfold.” The word “But” turns the story. Other seed falls into good ground and brings forth fruit, and the fruit comes in three amounts.'))
A(('p', '3.6 The call (13:9). “Who hath ears to hear, let him hear.” Jesus closes the telling of the story with this sentence, and Matthew records no other word from Jesus before the disciples come to Him in 13:10.'))
A(('p', '3.7 The turning points in Matthew. The story turns at “But other fell into good ground.” Before that turn there are three places where the seed falls and does not come to fruit, and each loss has its own cause in the telling: the fowls, the sun with no root, the thorns. After the turn, the seed that falls into good ground brings forth fruit, and the fruit is counted in three amounts. The narrative logic is one sowing, four places, three losses and one fruitful result.'))
A(('p', '3.8 Mark’s telling (Mark 4:3–9). Mark’s account is read on its own. “Hearken; Behold, there went out a sower to sow” (4:3). The way side: “as he sowed, some fell by the way side, and the fowls of the air came and devoured it up” (4:4). Stony ground: “where it had not much earth; and immediately it sprang up, because it had no depth of earth: But when the sun was up, it was scorched; and because it had no root, it withered away” (4:5–6). Thorns: “the thorns grew up, and choked it, and it yielded no fruit” (4:7). Good ground: “did yield fruit that sprang up and increased; and brought forth, some thirty, and some sixty, and some an hundred” (4:8). The close: “He that hath ears to hear, let him hear” (4:9). Mark opens with a word of address, “Hearken,” says of the choked seed that it “yielded no fruit,” and says of the good seed that it “sprang up and increased.”'))
A(('p', '3.9 Luke’s telling (Luke 8:5–8). Luke’s account is read on its own. “A sower went out to sow his seed” (8:5). The way side: “some fell by the way side; and it was trodden down, and the fowls of the air devoured it” (8:5). The rock: “And some fell upon a rock; and as soon as it was sprung up, it withered away, because it lacked moisture” (8:6). Thorns: “And some fell among thorns; and the thorns sprang up with it, and choked it” (8:7). Good ground: “And other fell on good ground, and sprang up, and bare fruit an hundredfold” (8:8). The close: “And when he had said these things, he cried, He that hath ears to hear, let him hear” (8:8). Luke names “his seed,” says the way-side seed “was trodden down,” gives the rock’s reason as “it lacked moisture,” and counts the fruit only as “an hundredfold.”'))
A(('p', '3.10 The three accounts side by side, by wording only. The table sets the wording of each account next to the others. It states no difference as a contradiction and draws no meaning from any difference.'))
