A(('p', '4.6 What Jesus does not name. Jesus does not identify the sower in Matthew or in Luke. In Mark He says “The sower soweth the word,” and the account does not say who the sower is. Jesus does not identify the fowls in the story by saying what they are. In Matthew He says “then cometh the wicked one,” in Mark “Satan cometh immediately,” and in Luke “then cometh the devil.” Each name is reported in its own account and the three are not merged, and Jesus does not say in so many words, “the fowls are” any of them. Jesus does not identify the sun. The explanation speaks of “tribulation or persecution” (Matthew 13:21), “affliction or persecution” (Mark 4:17) and “time of temptation” (Luke 8:13), and Jesus does not say, “the sun is” any of these. Jesus does not identify the thorns by saying “the thorns are”; He names what chokes the word in each account (“the care of this world, and the deceitfulness of riches” in Matthew; “the cares of this world, and the deceitfulness of riches, and the lusts of other things” in Mark; “cares and riches and pleasures of this life” in Luke). Jesus does not identify the root beyond saying that the one has “not root in himself.” Jesus does not identify the three amounts of fruit. Jesus does not say what the fruit is. Jesus does not identify the multitude, the places the seed fell or the sower’s field.'))
A(('call', 'A Warning Against Adding',
   'The identifications Jesus gives are the ones recorded in Matthew 13:19–23, Mark 4:14–20 and Luke 8:11–15, each in its own account. A reader who adds to them, saying that the sower is a certain person, that the fowls are a certain being, that the sun is a certain trial, or that the good ground is a certain kind of person made by a certain act, supplies what the Lord did not. The parable is read with the identifications He gives and no more.'))
A(('p', '4.7 The explanation stays with its account. The names “the wicked one,” “Satan” and “the devil” belong to Matthew, Mark and Luke respectively, and they are not made into one statement. The identifications are given for this parable. They are not given for the Wheat and the Weeds, where Jesus gives a different explanation (Matthew 13:37–39), and they are not given for the parables that follow in the same chapter.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 5
A(('h1', S5))
A(('p', 'This section states what the comparison itself says about the Kingdom’s word. It uses the parable’s own words, and it assigns no identity beyond those Jesus gave in Section 4.'))
A(('p', '5.1 The comparison. Matthew introduces the parable only as a sowing, “Behold, a sower went forth to sow” (13:3), and Jesus’ explanation refers to “the word of the kingdom” (Matthew 13:19), “the word” (Mark 4:14) and “the word of God” (Luke 8:11). The comparison is a whole situation: one sowing, seed that falls in four places, three places where it does not come to fruit and one where it does.'))
A(('p', '5.2 What the comparison communicates about the Kingdom’s word.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The same sowing meets different outcomes.** The sower goes out to sow, and the seed falls in four places. The story does not describe a poor sowing in some places and a good one in others. It describes the same sowing and what happened where the seed fell.',
    '**Hearing is not the end of the matter.** In all three accounts those who fall short are described as having heard (Matthew 13:19–22; Mark 4:15–18; Luke 8:12–14). The comparison shows that a word heard can still be taken away, can wither or can be choked.',
    '**The word can be lost in more than one way.** It is taken away, it fails for want of root when trouble rises, and it is choked by cares, riches and other things. The three losses are different in kind and are kept distinct.',
    '**Early gladness is not the same as fruit.** The one on stony ground receives the word “with joy” (Matthew 13:20), “with gladness” (Mark 4:16), and endures “for a while” or “but for a time.” The comparison separates quick reception from lasting fruit.',
    '**Fruit marks the good ground.** The good ground bears fruit, and the fruit is counted: “some an hundredfold, some sixty, some thirty” (Matthew 13:23). The comparison treats the fruit as the result that the sowing was for.',
    '**The call to hear closes the telling.** Jesus ends the story with “Who hath ears to hear, let him hear” (Matthew 13:9). The comparison puts weight on how the word is heard.',
]))
A(('p', '5.3 What the hearers were meant to understand. The hearers were people who had been told that the Kingdom of heaven is at hand (Matthew 4:17). They were meant to understand that the word of the Kingdom, though sown, does not meet one response. Some who hear lose it at once, some hold it briefly, some are choked by the cares and riches around them, and some bring forth fruit. They were also meant to understand that how the word is heard is weighed, and that Jesus presses this on them with the call, “He that hath ears to hear, let him hear” (Mark 4:9).'))
A(('p', '5.4 The force of the parable. The force is a warning held together with a hope. The warning is that the word of the Kingdom, once heard, can be taken, can wither or can be choked. The hope is that there is ground in which it bears fruit, and that the fruit is real and counted. The comparison says nothing of a sower who stops sowing, and it does not close with the loss. It closes with the fruit and the call to hear.'))
A(('p', '5.5 What the comparison supports, in short statements. A word can be heard and still lost. A word can be received with joy and still fail. What chokes the word may be among the ordinary things of this world. The word can bear fruit. These statements arise from the comparison itself, and they do not say that any group or object “represents” another beyond what Jesus said in Matthew 13:19–23, Mark 4:14–20 and Luke 8:11–15.'))
A(('p', '**What this section does not do.** It does not say who the sower is or who the fowls, the sun or the thorns are, because Jesus does not identify them. It does not say how a person comes to be good ground, and it does not say why the ground is as it is, because the text does not. It does not treat hearing, receiving, keeping or bearing fruit as a way of becoming saved or lost, and it does not draw a meaning from another parable into this one.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 6
A(('h1', S6))
A(('p', 'Matthew 13 gathers parables that look alike, and a reader moves easily from one to another. Each is kept in its own place here. A meaning given in one is not carried into another unless Jesus carries it.'))
A(('tbl', '', [
    ['Matt 13:24–30', '“Another parable put he forth unto them, saying, The kingdom of heaven is likened unto a man which sowed good seed in his field”', 'The Wheat and the Weeds. Jesus explains it in 13:37–39 with different identifications. There the sower is “the Son of man” and the field “the world.” Those identifications are not carried into the sower’s parable, and nothing here is carried into that one.'],
    ['Mark 4:26–29', '“So is the kingdom of God, as if a man should cast seed into the ground”', 'The Growing Seed. Seed is cast and grows “he knoweth not how.” It has no soils, no fowls, no thorns and no explanation from Jesus.'],
    ['Matt 13:31–33', '“The kingdom of heaven is like to a grain of mustard seed”', 'The Mustard Seed and the Leaven. A seed grows to a tree and leaven works through meal. There are no soils and no explanation from Jesus.'],
    ['Matt 13:44–46', '“The kingdom of heaven is like unto treasure hid in a field”', 'The Hidden Treasure and the Pearl. A field appears, and Jesus does not say what it is. A field in one parable is not carried into another.'],
    ['Matt 13:47–50', '“The kingdom of heaven is like unto a net, that was cast into the sea”', 'The Dragnet. A net gathers every kind, and a separation follows at the end. It has no soils and no seed.'],
], [0.17, 0.38, 0.45]))
A(('p', '6.1 Differences in setting. The sower’s parable is the first told on the day Matthew 13 describes, to the multitude, with the question and the explanation following in the account (Matthew 13:10–23). The Wheat and the Weeds is the next, and its explanation is given in the house after the multitude has gone (13:36). The Growing Seed is in Mark 4, after Jesus’ words about hearing (4:21–25). The shared chapter and the shared sea-side setting do not make these one parable.'))
A(('p', '6.2 Differences in characters. In the sower’s parable there is a sower, seed, the fowls, the sun, thorns and four kinds of ground. In the Wheat and the Weeds, there is a man, good seed, an enemy, tares, servants, a householder and reapers. In the Growing Seed, a man casts seed and sleeps and rises. In the Mustard Seed, a man takes a seed; in the Leaven, a woman takes leaven. Soils belong to the sower’s parable alone, and an enemy belongs to the Wheat and the Weeds alone.'))
A(('p', '6.3 Differences in narrative movement. The sower’s parable moves from one sowing to four places and their four results. The Wheat and the Weeds moves from a sowing to an enemy’s act, a refusal to separate and a harvest with an order. The Growing Seed moves from casting to a growth the sower does not understand to a harvest. The Mustard Seed moves from smallness to a tree. None of these movements is read into another.'))
A(('p', '6.4 Differences in interpretive detail. Four details must not be carried across. First, the seed of this parable is “the word of God” in Luke 8:11, and “the good seed” of the Wheat and the Weeds is “the children of the kingdom” in Matthew 13:38. The two explanations are not made into one. Second, “the wicked one” in Matthew 13:19 and “the devil” in 13:39 are each reported where they stand, and neither is placed into the other’s parable. Third, the field of Matthew 13:38 is not a field in this parable, and no field is named here. Fourth, the “fowls” are not carried into the “birds of the air” that lodge in the mustard tree (13:32); Jesus does not say what either is.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 7
A(('h1', S7))
A(('p', '7.1 The original address. Jesus speaks to the multitude on the shore and, afterward, to His disciples who ask Him, and He speaks during the earthly ministry in which He preached the Kingdom of heaven as at hand (Matthew 4:17) and sent the Twelve to “the lost sheep of the house of Israel” (10:6). Luke places the parable after He “went throughout every city and village, preaching and shewing the glad tidings of the kingdom of God” (Luke 8:1). The parable belongs to that ministry. The Kingdom it speaks of is the Kingdom Jesus announced to Israel.'))
A(('call', 'Standing Statements',
   'Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. This is a principled lesson, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body.'))
A(('p', '7.2 The Acts overlap boundary. Israel’s prophetic program did not end with the Gospels, and it did not stop when Paul was called. After the Gospels, the Kingdom message is still preached to Israel in Acts 1–8, and through Acts 9–28 Israel’s program and the Mystery run side by side. The two never merge, transfer or absorb one another. Israel’s program was nationally suspended at Acts 28. The parable was told before any of that, and it is not read as though the church of the Body were already present among the soils.'))
A(('p', '7.3 The Mystery begins with Paul. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. The “mysteries of the kingdom of heaven” that Jesus says are given to the disciples (Matthew 13:11), and the “mystery of the kingdom of God” of Mark 4:11, are His own Kingdom matters, given to them during His earthly ministry. They are not the Mystery that was hid in God and made known through Paul. The word is shared and the subject is not.'))
A(('p', '7.4 What is not transferred. The sower, the seed, the word of the kingdom, the soils and the fruit belong to the Kingdom Jesus announced to Israel. They are not made the identity or the salvation of the Body of Christ. The Body is not placed among the four soils, and the four soils are not a sorting of the Body. The actions in the parable, hearing, receiving, keeping and bearing fruit, are not read as the way anyone is saved, and Luke 8:12 is recorded as written in this parable’s setting and not turned into a statement of how the Body is saved. The Body’s salvation is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable: “For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast” (Ephesians 2:8–9).'))
A(('p', '7.5 Responsible present learning. A reader today may draw lessons from what the parable shows. They are lessons of conduct and not of identity.'))
A(('bul', [
    '**Attention to how the word is heard.** Jesus closes with a call to hear. A reader learns to treat what he hears as something that can be lost, and to give it the weight Jesus gives it.',
    '**Honesty about shallow gladness.** The word is received with joy and then fails when trouble comes. A reader learns not to mistake quick enthusiasm for lasting fruit.',
    '**Watchfulness over competing cares.** The cares, riches and pleasures named in the explanation choke the word. A reader learns to look at what crowds out what he has heard.',
    '**Patience in fruit.** Luke closes with fruit “with patience” (Luke 8:15). A reader learns that fruit takes time and is not hurried.',
    '**Humility about other persons.** Jesus does not tell the hearers to sort the soils among those around them. A reader learns not to declare what ground another person is.',
]))
A(('p', 'These are principled lessons, not a transfer of Kingdom identity to the Body. The parable’s word of the kingdom, its soils and its fruit are not read as the Body’s identity or destiny. Whatever the Body is told about its own calling is read in Paul’s letters and not in this parable.'))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 8
A(('h1', S8))
A(('p', 'A sower goes out to sow, and the seed falls in four places. By the way side the fowls devour it. On stony places it springs up at once, and when the sun is up it is scorched and withers for want of root. Among thorns it is choked. In good ground it brings forth fruit in three amounts. Jesus ends with a call to hear. In the house He explains each place in His own words: the one who hears the word and is robbed of it by the wicked one (Matthew), by Satan (Mark) or by the devil (Luke); the one who receives it with joy and has no root; the one in whom the cares of the world and the deceitfulness of riches choke it; and the one who hears, understands and bears fruit. He says the sower soweth the word (Mark 4:14), and the seed is the word of God (Luke 8:11). Everything else in the story He leaves unnamed. The comparison says that the word of the Kingdom, heard by many, meets loss in three ways and fruit in one. Jesus speaks during His earthly Kingdom ministry before the Acts overlap. The Mystery of the Body of Christ begins with Paul’s Acts 9 calling. Any reading that names the sower, the fowls or the sun, or that makes hearing and bearing fruit a way of being saved, says more than the text says.'))
A(('pk', '**Teaching Summary.**'))
A(('bul', [
    '**The sequence:** one sowing, four places, three losses and one fruitful result, closed by the call to hear.',
    '**The meaning:** the word of the Kingdom can be heard and lost, received and withered, or choked; it also bears fruit.',
    '**The boundaries:** only the identifications Jesus gives in each account; no names merged across the accounts; no symbol carried in from another parable; no group assigned to the unnamed parts; the Body is not among the soils.',
    '**The present lesson:** attention to how the word is heard, honesty about shallow gladness, watchfulness over competing cares and patience in fruit, drawn as principles and not as a transfer.',
]))

